The pentagon \(ABCDE\) has internal angles \(\angle A = \angle E = 90^\circ\), \(\angle B = \angle D = 45^\circ\), and \(\angle C = 270^\circ\) (since the sum of interior angles is \(540^\circ\)). Placing \(A = (0,0)\), \(E = (20,0)\), \(B = (0,b)\) (with \(b = AB\) integer), and \(D = (20,d)\) (with \(d = DE\)) on the same side of line \(AE\) (taken as the positive \(y\)-direction), the conditions at \(B\) and \(D\) lead to \(C = (14, b-14)\) with \(d = b-8\). The requirement that \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\) are on the same side of \(AE\) gives \(b-14 > 0\) or \(b > 14\). The area of the pentagon computed via the shoelace formula is \(10b - 84\).

Given \(AB < 2026\), the area must be an integer multiple of \(16\), so \(10b - 84 \equiv 0 \pmod{16}\). This simplifies to \(b \equiv 2 \pmod{8}\). Combining with \(b > 14\) and \(b < 2026\), the smallest such \(b\) is \(18\) and the largest is \(2018\). The number of integers of the form \(b = 8k+2\) with \(k = 2, 3, \ldots, 252\) is \(252 - 2 + 1 = 251\).

