The debt process follows the recurrence \(D_n = 2(D_{n-1} - X_n)\), where \(X_n\) is 1 or 2 with equal probability, starting from \(D_0 = \pi\). Derek becomes debt-free if at some step \(n\), \(D_{n-1} - X_n \leq 0\), which is equivalent to \(D_n \leq 0\).

Expressing \(D_n\) as \(D_n = 2^n (\pi - A_n)\) with \(A_n = \sum_{k=1}^n X_k / 2^{k-1}\), the condition becomes \(A_n \geq \pi\). Since \(A_n\) increases almost surely to \(A_\infty = \sum_{k=1}^\infty X_k / 2^{k-1}\), which is uniformly distributed on \([2, 4]\), the probability that there exists an \(n\) with \(A_n \geq \pi\) is \(P(A_\infty > \pi) = (4 - \pi)/2\).

\boxed{\dfrac{4-\pi}{2}}

The vertices of the prism consist of two regular decagons: a top base \(A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_{10}\) and a bottom base \(B_1, B_2, \ldots, B_{10}\). A plane that does not pass through any vertex partitions the vertices into two sets, and \(M\) is one of these sets. The set \(M\) is uniquely determined by the subsets \(T \subseteq \{A_1, \ldots, A_{10}\}\) and \(B \subseteq \{B_1, \ldots, B_{10}\}\) of vertices on the same side of the plane as \(M\). Geometric considerations show that \(T\) and \(B\) must be arcs (including the empty and full sets) of the respective decagons, and they must be nested: either \(T \subseteq B\) or \(B \subseteq T\).

The number of arcs for a decagon (including empty and full) is 92. Counting nested pairs \((T, B)\) yields 3574 such pairs. Excluding the cases where \(M\) is empty or the entire vertex set (i.e., \((T, B) = (\emptyset, \emptyset)\) or \((T, B) = (\text{full}, \text{full})\)) gives 3572 possible sets \(M\).

\boxed{3572}

The vertices of the right prism are partitioned by a plane into two subsets, one of which is \(M\). The vertices lie on two parallel regular decagons, and the plane determines two parallel lines, one on each base. The subsets of vertices on each base that lie on one side of the plane are contiguous blocks (cyclic intervals) in the ordering induced by the projection direction. The pair \((M_b, M_t)\), where \(M_b\) and \(M_t\) are the subsets on the bottom and top bases, is realizable if and only if both are cyclic intervals (including empty and full) and, when both are nonempty and proper, one is contained in the other and is an endpoint subinterval.

Counting such pairs yields \(1894\) for a decagonal prism (\(n = 10\)). This result aligns with the general formula \(n(n-1)(2n+1) + 4\) for an \(n\)-gonal prism.

\boxed{1894}

